   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>10</x><y>187</y><width>261</width><height>16</height></rect></property>
   <property name="text"><string>Selected: —</string></property>
  </widget>

  <!-- COLUMN 2: ALGORITHM + SEARCH WINDOW -->
  <widget class="QLabel" name="label_algorithm">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>290</x><y>10</y><width>300</width><height>16</height></rect></property>
   <property name="font"><font><weight>75</weight><bold>true</bold></font></property>
   <property name="text"><string>Water quality algorithm (see ⓘ for each)</string></property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioBtn_algo_ndci">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>300</x><y>32</y><width>250</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="text"><string>Chlorophyll-a proxy (NDCI)</string></property>
   <property name="checked"><bool>true</bool></property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QLabel" name="icon_algo_ndci">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>555</x><y>32</y><width>16</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="font"><font><bold>true</bold></font></property>
   <property name="styleSheet"><string>color: #0066cc;</string></property>
   <property name="text"><string>ⓘ</string></property>
   <property name="toolTip">
    <string>NDCI — Normalized Difference Chlorophyll Index, a quick unitless chlorophyll-a proxy needing no calibration.

(B05-B04)/(B05+B04) — Red Edge vs. Red reflectance contrast. Higher values suggest more chlorophyll-a and greater algal bloom potential.

Reference: Mishra &amp; Mishra (2012), Remote Sensing of Environment 117.

Relative screening proxy, not a calibrated mg/m³ concentration — for an absolute estimate see Gilerson below.</string>
   </property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioBtn_algo_ndti">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>300</x><y>54</y><width>250</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="text"><string>Turbidity proxy (NDTI)</string></property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QLabel" name="icon_algo_ndti">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>555</x><y>54</y><width>16</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="font"><font><bold>true</bold></font></property>
   <property name="styleSheet"><string>color: #0066cc;</string></property>
   <property name="text"><string>ⓘ</string></property>
   <property name="toolTip">
    <string>NDTI — Normalized Difference Turbidity Index, a quick unitless turbidity proxy needing no calibration.

(B04-B03)/(B04+B03) — Red vs. Green reflectance contrast; Red brightens as suspended sediment increases. Higher values suggest higher turbidity and lower water clarity.

Reference: Lacaux et al. (2007), Remote Sensing of Environment 106(1).

Relative screening proxy, not a calibrated NTU value.</string>
   </property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioBtn_algo_oc3">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>300</x><y>76</y><width>250</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="text"><string>Clear-water chlorophyll (OC3)</string></property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QLabel" name="icon_algo_oc3">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>555</x><y>76</y><width>16</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="font"><font><bold>true</bold></font></property>
   <property name="styleSheet"><string>color: #0066cc;</string></property>
   <property name="text"><string>ⓘ</string></property>
   <property name="toolTip">
    <string>OC3 — a blue/green band-ratio chlorophyll-a algorithm for clear, low-turbidity water.

Uses max(B01,B02)/B03 (Blue vs. Green): chlorophyll absorbs blue light, so the ratio falls as chlorophyll-a rises.

Best for clear lakes — sediment/CDOM in turbid water can throw this off; use Gilerson for turbid lakes instead.

Reference: O'Reilly et al. (1998), Journal of Geophysical Research 103(C11).

Coefficients are published example values, not calibrated for a specific lake — treat as a relative screening signal, not an accurate mg/m³ reading, until calibrated against in-situ samples.</string>
   </property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioBtn_algo_gilerson">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>300</x><y>98</y><width>250</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="text"><string>Turbid-water chlorophyll (Gilerson)</string></property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QLabel" name="icon_algo_gilerson">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>555</x><y>98</y><width>16</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="font"><font><bold>true</bold></font></property>
   <property name="styleSheet"><string>color: #0066cc;</string></property>
   <property name="text"><string>ⓘ</string></property>
   <property name="toolTip">
    <string>Gilerson — a red/red-edge band-ratio chlorophyll-a algorithm for turbid, algae-rich water.

Uses B05/B04 (Red Edge vs. Red): Red sits in chlorophyll's strongest absorption band, while Red Edge rises with algal biomass scattering — the ratio tracks chlorophyll-a even when suspended sediment is also present.

Best for turbid inland/coastal lakes — for clear open water use OC3 instead.

Reference: Gilerson et al. (2010), Optics Express 18(23).

Coefficients are published example values, not calibrated for a specific lake — treat as a relative screening signal, not an accurate mg/m³ reading, until calibrated against in-situ samples.</string>
   </property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QLabel" name="label_extraDays">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>290</x><y>128</y><width>250</width><height>16</height></rect></property>
   <property name="font"><font><weight>75</weight><bold>true</bold></font></property>
   <property name="text"><string>Search window (±days)</string></property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QSpinBox" name="spinBox_extraDays">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>300</x><y>150</y><width>80</width><height>22</height></rect></property>
   <property name="minimum"><number>1</number></property>
   <property name="maximum"><number>180</number></property>
   <property name="value"><number>15</number></property>
  </widget>

  <!-- STUDY AREA -->
  <widget class="QLabel" name="label_studyArea">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>10</x><y>215</y><width>300</width><height>16</height></rect></property>
   <property name="font"><font><weight>75</weight><bold>true</bold></font></property>
   <property name="text"><string>Select study area</string></property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QLabel" name="icon_studyArea_tiles">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>140</x><y>215</y><width>16</width><height>16</height></rect></property>
   <property name="font"><font><bold>true</bold></font></property>
   <property name="styleSheet"><string>color: #0066cc;</string></property>
   <property name="text"><string>ⓘ</string></property>
   <property name="toolTip">
    <string>Each search returns exactly one scene — a Sentinel-2 tile is roughly 110×110 km. If your area is bigger than that, or straddles a tile boundary, the plugin picks the scene with the largest overlap with your area (not just the least cloudy one) and warns you if the result still doesn't fully cover it.</string>
   </property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioBtn_aoi_draw">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>10</x><y>236</y><width>130</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="text"><string>Draw on map</string></property>
   <property name="checked"><bool>true</bool></property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QRadioButton" name="radioBtn_aoi_layer">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>150</x><y>236</y><width>90</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="text"><string>From layer</string></property>
  </widget>
  <widget class="QLabel" name="icon_aoi_layer_crs">
   <property name="geometry"><rect><x>240</x><y>236</y><width>16</width><height>18</height></rect></property>
   <property name="font"><font><bold>true</bold></font></property>
   <property name="styleSheet"><string>color: #0066cc;</string></property>
   <property name="text"><string>ⓘ</string></property>
   <property name="toolTip">
    <string>Any CRS works — the layer's coordinate reference system doesn't matter. Its features are automatically reprojected to WGS-84 (EPSG:4326) before searching, whatever projection the layer is currently in.</string>
   </property>
  </widget>

